"doc": "DS3_2024-2025_Informatique3-DS_P2S1_DInformatique",
"title": "DS3",
"doc_label": "DS3 · 2024-2025",
"fmt": "text",
"section": "Exercice 4",
"label": "DS3 · 2024-2025 — Exercice 4",
"text": "Exercice 4 ( Shell : Débit d’attractions d’un fameux parc Allemand (6 pt) )\nUn parc d’attraction souhaite faire le point sur le débit horaire (nombre de personnes/heure à embarquer dans une\nattraction) de ses montagnes russes.\nL’objectif de cet exercice est d’écrire un script shell permettant d’analyser les statistiques de fréquentation des attractions\nqui sont reportées dans un fichierattractions.txt. Chacune des lignes de ce fichier est au format suivant :\n<nom_attraction>-<nombre_personnes>-<heures_ouvertes>\nExemple de fichierattractions.txt :\nVoltron-14400-8\nSilverStar-15300-9\nBlueFire-9600-8\nPoseidon-0-0\nWodan-14000-10\nÀ partir de ce fichier, écrire un script Shell qui prendra en paramètre le nom d’un fichier au format indiqué ci-dessus.\nUne documentation de différentes commandes est disponible à la fin de ce sujet.\n1. Vérifier le nombre d’arguments et la validité de ceux-ci. Afficher des messages d’erreur appropriés en cas de problème.\nSi le fichier n’existe pas ou n’est pas accessible, signaler l’erreur et arrêter le script.\n2. Afficher uniquement le nom de toutes les attractions listées dans le fichier.\nAide pour la suite : l’instruction suivante\nfor ligne in $(cat \"fichier\")\npermet de parcourir chaque ligne defichier.\n3. Afficher la ligne complète de l’attraction ayant accueilli le plus de personnes.\n4. Identifier les attractions ayant0 en nombre de personnes et en heures ouvertes (les attractions fermées). Générer un\nnouveau fichier coasters_fonctionnels.txt comportant uniquement les informations des attractions ouvertes.\n5. Générer un nouveau fichierdebit.txt contenant les informations complètes des attractions ouvertes, accompagnées\ndu débit horaire entier (calculé commenombre_personnes / heures_ouvertes ), formaté comme suit :\n<nom_attraction>;<nombre_personnes>;<heures_ouvertes>;<debit_horaire>\n6. Parmis les attraction fonctionnelles, afficher les deux attractions ayant le meilleur débit horaire et les deux attractions\nayant le moins bon débit horaire.\n3\n\nDOCUMENT A TION\n-\ncut - remove sections from each line of files\nSYNOPSIS\ncut OPTION... [FILE]...\nDESCRIPTION top\nPrint selected parts of lines from each FILE to standard output. With no FILE, or when\nFILE is -, read standard input. Mandatory arguments to long options are mandatory for\nshort options too.\n-b, --bytes=LIST\nselect only these bytes\n-c, --characters=LIST\nselect only these characters\n-d, --delimiter=DELIM\nuse DELIM instead of TAB for field delimiter\n-f, --fields=LIST\nselect only these fields; also print any line that contains no delimiter\ncharacter, unless the -s option is specified\n--output-delimiter=STRING use STRING as the output delimiter\nthe default is to use the input delimiter\n————————————————-\nhead - Print the first 10 lines of each FILE to standard output.\nUsage: head [OPTION]... [FILE]...\nWith more than one FILE, precede each with a header giving the file name.\nWith no FILE, or when FILE is -, read standard input.\nMandatory arguments to long options are mandatory for short options too.\n-c, --bytes=[-]NUM print the first NUM bytes of each file;\nwith the leading '-', print all but the last\nNUM bytes of each file\n-n, --lines=[-]NUM print the first NUM lines instead of the first 10;\nwith the leading '-', print all but the last\nNUM lines of each file\n————————————————-\ntail - Print the last 10 lines of each FILE to standard output.\nUsage: tail [OPTION]... [FILE]...\nWith more than one FILE, precede each with a header giving the file name.\nWith no FILE, or when FILE is -, read standard input.\nMandatory arguments to long options are mandatory for short options too.\n-c, --bytes=[+]NUM output the last NUM bytes; or use -c +NUM to\noutput starting with byte NUM of each file\n-n, --lines=[+]NUM output the last NUM lines, instead of the last 10;\nor use -n +NUM to output starting with line NUM\n--max-unchanged-stats=N\nwith --follow=name, reopen a FILE which has not\nchanged size after N (default 5) iterations\nto see if it has been unlinked or renamed\n(this is the usual case of rotated log files);\nwith inotify, this option is rarely useful\n--pid=PID with -f, terminate after process ID, PID dies\n4\n\n————————————————-\ngrep - print lines that match patterns\nSYNOPSIS\ngrep [OPTION...] PATTERNS [FILE...]\ngrep [OPTION...] -e PATTERNS ... [FILE...]\ngrep [OPTION...] -f PATTERN_FILE ... [FILE...]\nDESCRIPTION\ngrep searches for PATTERNS in each FILE. PATTERNS is one or patterns separated by\nnewline characters, and grep prints each line that matches a pattern.\nOPTIONS\nMatching Control\n-f FILE, --file=FILE\nObtain patterns from FILE, one per line. If this option is used multiple times\nor is combined with the -e (--regexp) option, search for all patterns given.\nThe empty file contains zero patterns, and therefore matches nothing.\n-i, --ignore-case\nIgnore case distinctions, characters that differ only in case match each other.\n-v, --invert-match\nInvert the sense of matching, to select non-matching lines.\nOutput Line Prefix Control\n-n, --line-number\nPrefix each line of output with the 1-based line number\nwithin its input file.\n————————————————-\nsort - sort lines of text files\nDESCRIPTION\n-d, --dictionary-order\nconsider only blanks and alphanumeric characters\n-f, --ignore-case\nfold lower case to upper case characters\n-g, --general-numeric-sort\ncompare according to general numerical value\n-n, --numeric-sort\ncompare according to string numerical value\n-r, --reverse\nreverse the result of comparisons\n--sort=WORD\nsort according to WORD: general-numeric -g, human-numeric\n-h, month -M, numeric -n, random -R, version -V\n————————————————-\ntr - translate or delete characters\nSYNOPSIS\ntr [OPTION]... STRING1 [STRING2]\nDESCRIPTION\nTranslate, squeeze, and/or delete characters from standard input,\nwriting to standard output. STRING1 and STRING2 specify arrays\nof characters ARRAY1 and ARRAY2 that control the action.\n-d, --delete\ndelete characters in ARRAY1, do not translate\n-s, --squeeze-repeats\nreplace each sequence of a repeated character that is\nlisted in the last specified ARRAY, with a single\noccurrence of that character\n[:digit:]\nall digits\n[:lower:]\nall lower case letters\n[:upper:]\nall upper case letters\n5"
},
